{"description":"M1 — the only specification change in this plan, and therefore the only item that inherits a standard version: 1.4 is spent, so this lands in 1.5. std-, spec-, adr- and req- may declare the paths or modules they bind, and eka context --for \u003cpath\u003e then returns the constraint set deterministically, one hop, no embeddings: the higher-stratum artifacts that bind that file. Today the question 'which standard binds this change' has no machine answer, because no artifact states what it applies to, so an agent cannot call the right constraint even when it wants to. Granularity is the open risk: too wide and the gate is noise an agent learns to ignore, too narrow and the standard never activates; the first version therefore ships with an explicit, reviewable declaration syntax and a spike measuring noise on real tasks.","acceptanceCriteria":"- The standard corpus declares the binding field additively in 1.5, with the freeze boundary respected (1.4 is not edited).\n- eka context --for \u003cpath\u003e returns exactly the constraints whose declared scope matches the path, deterministically ordered, in both human and --json form; a path matching nothing returns an empty set rather than an error.\n- Scope declarations are validated: a malformed glob is a blocking validation failure, and a declaration that matches no path in the repository is a warning.\n- eka-core accepts 1.4 packages unchanged while declaring 1.5, proven by the accepted-version test widened the same way it was for 1.4.\n- The noise measurement is recorded: on a sample of changes in this repository, how many constraints a typical file pulls in, so the granularity decision has evidence rather than taste."}